SEREN — SETTINGS SYSTEM & SETTINGS MENU IMPLEMENTATION

Work directly from the current Seren repository and implement a complete Settings system that feels native to the game’s recently redesigned interface.

Before editing anything, inspect the current implementation in:

* index.html
* css/app.css
* js/main.js
* js/ui.js
* js/i18n.js
* js/audio.js
* js/core.js
* any other file that references language, sound, accessibility, motion, contrast, menu focus, localStorage, or the home navigation

Do not build an isolated settings feature that duplicates systems already present in the project. Integrate with and improve the existing architecture.

The final result must feel like Seren always had a proper Settings menu.

⸻

PRIMARY GOAL

Replace the current standalone audio toggle on the Home screen with a Settings button.

The Settings button must occupy the exact same UI position currently occupied by the sound/audio button.

Do not move the Home screen’s primary content or redesign the whole Home screen around this feature.

The Settings button opens a polished Settings interface containing:

1. Language
2. Audio
3. Accessibility / interface preferences
4. Other useful player preferences that genuinely affect the game
5. A safe way to restore settings to their defaults

Do not fill the Settings menu with fake or non-functional options.

Every visible setting must actually work.

⸻

HOME SCREEN CHANGES

The current Home screen has separate Language and Sound controls.

Consolidate these preferences into the new Settings system.

Replace the current Sound button

Remove the existing direct sound-toggle behavior from the Home topbar.

The current #btnSound position becomes the Settings button.

Use an appropriate minimal settings icon such as sliders or a gear, but draw it using the same inline SVG conventions already used throughout Seren.

The button should:

* visually use the existing .icon-btn language
* have the same dimensions as the old button
* have an accessible translated aria-label
* open Settings
* work with keyboard navigation
* retain proper focus behavior after the Settings menu is closed

Use an ID such as:

#btnSettings

Do not retain the old sound icon in this location.

Remove the redundant standalone Language control

Because Language will now live inside Settings, remove the permanent #btnLang control from the Home screen.

Do not remove Seren’s first-launch language selection behavior.

On a fresh installation where no language has been chosen yet, Seren should still ask the player to select English or French before normal interaction.

After that initial choice, language changes happen through Settings.

The Home topbar should therefore become substantially cleaner, with Settings being the main utility control.

⸻

SETTINGS PRESENTATION

Create a dedicated settings overlay/dialog rather than turning Settings into another race-setup screen.

Suggested structure:

#settingsWrap
→ #settingsCard
→ Settings header
→ scrollable settings content
→ grouped settings sections

It should use role="dialog" and aria-modal="true".

The visual treatment must follow Seren’s established design system:

* black / near-black foundation
* white primary typography
* gray secondary typography
* red as the sole interface accent
* thin translucent borders
* glass / semi-transparent elevated surface
* restrained blur
* subtle depth
* minimal ornamentation
* Archivo display typography for major headings
* IBM Plex Sans / Mono where currently appropriate
* existing spacing and token system from app.css

Do not introduce blue, green, purple, gradients unrelated to Seren’s existing glass surfaces, generic Material UI styling, or large colorful preference icons.

This should look like a premium racing game’s settings panel, not a browser settings page.

⸻

SETTINGS LAYOUT

At the top:

SETTINGS

Include a clear Close button in the upper-right area of the panel.

Below it, organize preferences into clearly separated groups.

Use hierarchy through typography, spacing, borders, and subtle background changes rather than giant cards around every individual option.

Recommended sections:

GENERAL

Language

Show the currently selected language clearly.

Currently supported languages are:

* English
* Français

Do not advertise unsupported languages.

Use either a compact segmented selector or two clean language options.

Changing language must immediately update the entire visible interface without requiring a reload.

Reuse Seren’s existing localization system rather than introducing a second translation mechanism.

Continue storing the selected language using the existing:

seren.lang

The settings panel itself must also update immediately after the language changes.

Do not close Settings automatically simply because the language changed.

Refactor the existing language-selection logic into a reusable function if necessary so that both:

* first-launch language selection
* Settings language selection

