⸻

AUDIO

Seren currently has generated WebAudio effects and a global soundOn state.

Preserve that architecture.

Master sound

Provide a real On / Off switch.

It must control the same global audio state currently controlled by setSound().

Continue supporting the existing preference key:

seren.sound

Turning audio off must:

* mute all generated game audio
* stop the running engine sound as the game already does
* update Settings immediately

Turning audio back on must restore audio using the player’s configured volume.

Do not create a separate audio state that can become inconsistent with soundOn.

Master volume

Add a master-volume setting.

Use a range slider from:

0 → 100

Show the numerical percentage beside or near the slider.

Persist this separately, for example:

seren.volume

Use a sensible default corresponding approximately to Seren’s current master gain.

The existing audio code currently gives the master gain a fixed value when sound is enabled. Refactor that behavior so the gain becomes:

sound disabled:
0

sound enabled:
configured master volume

Changing volume should take effect immediately.

Do not create individual Music / Dialogue / Voice sliders because Seren does not currently have those audio systems.

Only expose settings corresponding to actual game audio.

The slider must support mouse, touch and keyboard input.

⸻

ACCESSIBILITY & INTERFACE

Add a compact Accessibility / Interface section.

These preferences must affect actual behavior.

Reduced motion

Seren already contains support for the operating system’s prefers-reduced-motion preference and also has JavaScript behavior associated with reduced motion.

Turn this into a proper player preference without breaking system-level accessibility.

Use three states:

* System
* Reduced
* Full

Default:

System

Persist something such as:

seren.motion

Behavior:

System
Use the operating system’s prefers-reduced-motion value.

Reduced
Force Seren’s reduced-motion behavior even when the operating system does not request it.

Full
Allow Seren’s normal animations even when the operating system requests reduced motion, provided doing so does not compromise core accessibility functionality.

Inspect every use of the existing NO_MOTION behavior before changing it.

Do not only stop CSS animations while leaving frame-by-frame JavaScript motion unaffected.

The setting needs to be applied coherently across both CSS and JS where Seren currently reacts to reduced motion.

Avoid scattering independent checks everywhere. Prefer a small centralized helper/state such as:

motionReduced()

or an equivalent clean implementation.

The setting must take effect without a page reload whenever reasonably possible.

⸻

HIGH CONTRAST

Seren already contains styling for prefers-contrast: more.

Expose this as another accessibility preference.

Use:

* System
* On
* Off

Persist something such as:

seren.contrast

System should continue respecting the browser/OS preference.

On should apply Seren’s existing higher-contrast treatment manually.

Off should use the standard visual treatment.

Do not redesign the color palette.

High Contrast should simply strengthen:

* text contrast
* borders
* glass separation
* interactive-state visibility

while retaining Seren’s black / white / gray / red identity.

⸻

CONTROL HINTS

Add:

Show control hints

This affects the desktop control-hint row displayed on the Home screen.

Default:

On.

Persist something such as:

seren.controlHints

When disabled:

* hide the keyboard control-hint group
* do not hide essential buttons
* do not affect gameplay controls
* do not affect controller instructions where those instructions are required to configure local multiplayer

This preference is purely about optional Home-screen hints.

The setting should update immediately.

⸻

RESTORE DEFAULT SETTINGS

At the bottom of Settings, provide a low-emphasis:

Restore default settings

This must reset preferences only.

Reset:

* language only if you deliberately decide that is appropriate
* sound enabled state
* volume
* motion preference
* contrast preference
* control-hint preference
* any other preference introduced by this Settings implementation

Do not erase:

* personal best
* game progress
* race records
* gameplay data unrelated to preferences

Preferably keep Language unchanged during a normal “Restore default settings” operation so the user does not suddenly lose the language they are currently using.

If language remains unchanged, make that behavior intentional.

Do not use localStorage.clear().

Reset only specific Seren preference keys.

Provide a lightweight confirmation before destructive preference reset if needed, but do not create an unnecessarily elaborate confirmation flow.

⸻

SETTINGS STATE ARCHITECTURE

Do not scatter direct calls to localStorage throughout event handlers.

Seren already has its store abstraction.

Continue using it.

Create centralized settings defaults and small read/write helpers where useful.

For example, conceptually:

SETTINGS_DEFAULTS

and helpers such as:

getSetting()
setSetting()
applySettings()

This does not have to be a massive new framework.

Keep the architecture appropriate to the current vanilla-JavaScript project.

The important requirement is to avoid having DOM state, stored state, and game state drift apart.

There should be one authoritative value for each preference.

⸻

AUDIO REFACTOR REQUIREMENTS

Preserve the existing lazy AudioContext behavior.

Do not create an AudioContext simply during page initialization if the browser would reject it.

The audio system currently intentionally creates WebAudio from a user interaction.

Maintain that safety.

Refactor master gain cleanly.

Conceptually:

effectiveGain = soundOn ? configuredVolume : 0

Any engine/audio node already connected through the master node should automatically respect the master volume.

Do not individually multiply volume into every sound-effect function unless absolutely necessary.

⸻

FIRST-LAUNCH LANGUAGE EXPERIENCE

Do not break the existing first-run language flow.

If seren.lang has never been chosen:

1. splash completes
2. Home becomes available behind the modal
3. the language chooser appears
4. focus is correctly trapped inside it
5. choosing a language saves it
6. the interface updates
7. the initial picker closes
8. focus returns correctly

Once language has been selected, this first-run modal should not appear again.

Language subsequently lives in Settings.

It is acceptable to keep the existing #langWrap specifically for this onboarding case instead of trying to make it serve two visually different jobs.

⸻

MODAL & FOCUS MANAGEMENT

The current menu code contains centralized focus gating.

Update it properly.

Settings must become a first-class modal alongside the existing language, pause and race-over panels.

When Settings opens:

* all underlying menu screens become inert
* focus moves into Settings
* keyboard Tab remains inside Settings
* Shift+Tab wraps correctly
* Escape closes Settings
* clicking the explicit Close button closes Settings
* underlying Home buttons cannot be activated

When Settings closes:

* inert state is removed correctly
* focus returns to #btnSettings
* the Home screen resumes normal keyboard navigation

Do not implement a second independent focus-trap system if the existing gateFocus() / menuKeydown() system can be extended.

Update modal detection centrally.

Avoid logic such as a growing chain of unrelated hardcoded conditions if a helper like:

activeModal()

makes the code easier to maintain.

For example, Settings, language, pause, and results should all be discoverable through the same central mechanism.

⸻

ESCAPE BEHAVIOR

Escape while Settings is open must close Settings.

Escape must not accidentally navigate from Home into another menu at the same time.

One keypress = one action.

If another modal is open, preserve that modal’s existing behavior.

⸻

